  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   We adopt a multi-phase optimization strategy that combines quasi-Newton methods (L-BFGS-B) from scipy.optimize with a domain-aware surrogate function. The core innovation is a piecewise polynomial basis representation that enforces the volume constraint directly, along with a derived surrogation rule exploiting the smoothness of optimal solutions near the optimum. This approach mitigates the curse of dimensionality by adaptively adjusting n_points while maintaining mathematical rigor. The method focuses on two key distances: the overlap integral and the Hamming distance in piecewise linear space, enabling hierarchical optimization from coarse to fine grids.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   Representation: Functions are represented by piecewise linear interpolations through design points, with transitions at 0.5 guided by Voronoi diagrams.\n   Variables: Basis functions with n_nodes = n_points \u00f7 4, where n_points is determined algorithmically.\n   Objective: Minimize max_k (\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx) [0,2] -> [0,1]\n   Constraint: \u222b h(x) dx = 1, 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n   Formulation: Use Chebyshev polynomials with cleared leading terms for exact volume enforcement. The correlation integral is computed via adaptive FFT with automatic padding.\n   Surrogate: Projects local optima onto a piecewise linear manifold before evaluation, ensuring feasibility.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   Phase 1: Coarse grid (n_points=64)\n     - Initialize with initial_h_values or thue-morse sequence\n     - Perform 30-step simplex search\n     - Repair via Chebyshev projection to satisfy box constraints\n\n   Phase 2: Medium grid (n_points=256)\n     - Upsample with cubic interpolation\n     - Apply L-BFGS-B optimization to surrogate\n     - Adaptive volume enforcement via gradient steps\n\n   Phase 3: Fine grid (n_points=512)\n     - Final refinement using successive Halley's method\n     - Terminal check against C=0.3808 threshold\n\n   Phase 4: Periodic restart policy (1/6th runtime)\n     - Randomized perturbation of current optimum\n     - Temperature-controlled resampling\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Primary grid progression: 64 -> 256 -> 512 (3 stages)\n   - Surrogate update frequency: Automatic based on grid resolution\n   - Temperature schedule: T(t) = T_max * np.exp(-t/\u03c4) with \u03c4=30s\n   - Basis function adjustment: Minimal twice per grid refinement\n\n5. Numerical Robustness and Memory Use\n   - Memory: Two active grids at any time (64+x-512 resolution)\n   - Failure recovery: \n     - Project_onto_feasible(volumes) function\n     - NaN detection in correlation calculations\n   - Dimension handling: Design points.T are allocated as (n_features, n_nodes) with lazy vectorization\n\n6. Runtime Safeguards and Return Contract\n   - Strict time budget enforcement: budget_s/total_expected_steps division\n   - Gradient clipping enforced by projection\n   - Multi-resolution fallback system\n   - Best candidate persistence across grid promotions\n\n   Helper functions integrate with the provided project_onto_box_sum and correlation scaling. The complete program will use 8 basis functions at each grid stage, with adaptive resolution increases controlled by the discrepancy between current best and record achievers.\n\n   Careful indexing handles all multi-dimensional projections inherently via basis transformations. Constraint enforcement is built into the interpolation structure, ensuring boundary conditions at 0/2 are naturally incorporated.\n\n   The optimizer will compute the exact overlap at candidate points, ensuring no convergence to infeasible local minima.",
